# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 5 (paper §9, §10)

@chapter 5 Where the Correction Lands
@transition iris 900
@bg terminal
@tone night
@show Vector right
@show Consumer left
Back indoors, if this is indoors. One screen. The Consumer is already at his post, and the Vector takes its place beside him as if for a photograph.
# src §9: "The following thought experiment connects selective invariance to change within an embedding. It specifies what different outcomes would establish; it reports no experimental results."
Page: The following thought experiment connects selective invariance to change within an embedding. It specifies what different outcomes would establish; it reports no experimental results. ^§9
# src §9.1: "Let the query program return whether an integer is positive. Its renamed version changes only the bound variable; its altered version instead returns whether the integer is negative."
Page: Let the query program return whether an integer is positive. Its renamed version changes only the bound variable; its altered version instead returns whether the integer is negative. ^§9.1
# src §9.1, Table 2: Query "lambda x: x > 0", Renamed "lambda n: n > 0", Altered "lambda x: x < 0".
@code python | lambda x: x > 0 | lambda n: n > 0 | lambda x: x < 0
# src §9.1: "Suppose the encoder initially places both candidates above the query's retrieval threshold."
Consumer (smile): Both candidates are above my threshold. I return them both. ^para
# src §9.1: "The renamed program's selection is appropriate; the altered program's selection is not."
Page: The renamed program’s selection is appropriate; the altered program’s selection is not. ^§9.1
Consumer (puzzled): Somebody tested them. On zero. They agreed on zero.
# src §9.1, Table 2: Query: input 0 False, input 2 True; Renamed: False, True; Altered: False, False. Caption: "A test on zero alone misses the difference."
@card Outputs on inputs 0 and 2 | Query: 0 → False, 2 → True | Renamed: 0 → False, 2 → True | Altered: 0 → False, 2 → False ^§9.1
# src §9.1: "An evaluation using only input zero would nevertheless accept both. The same observed agreement is compatible with behavioral tracking and with a similarity rule that ignores the comparison operator. A nonzero input separates these explanations."
Page: An evaluation using only input zero would nevertheless accept both. The same observed agreement is compatible with behavioral tracking and with a similarity rule that ignores the comparison operator. A nonzero input separates these explanations. ^§9.1
@move Consumer center
@show Checker left (neutral)
# src §9.2: "Give one learning arrangement diagnostic execution pairs, such as the different outputs on input 2" / "a checker that independently executes the programs"
Checker: I executed them, independently. On input 2 the outputs are different. ^para
@fx shake
# src §9.1: "It defeats an unqualified claim that this retrieval geometry preserves behavioral equivalence on D."
Page: It defeats an unqualified claim that this retrieval geometry preserves behavioral equivalence on D. ^§9.1
Vector (worried): Then there is nothing about behaviour in me. I am wording all the way through.
# src §9.1: "It does not show that the vector contains no behavioral information: that information might be present but unused by the chosen metric. Nor does one failure erase an established representational function."
Page: It does not show that the vector contains no behavioral information: that information might be present but unused by the chosen metric. Nor does one failure erase an established representational function. ^§9.1
# src §9.1: "Training history and consumer use may support describing the erroneous score as a misrepresentation of equivalence. The error identifies a boundary of competence that further interventions must locate."
Page: Training history and consumer use may support describing the erroneous score as a misrepresentation of equivalence. The error identifies a boundary of competence that further interventions must locate. ^§9.1
Vector (thinking): A misrepresentation. So I was saying something after all, and it was false.
(It sounds almost pleased. I let it have that.)
You: The evidence has arrived. The paper counts four things that can happen next.
@set seen = 0

== outcomes
* (once) Nothing changes. -> o_none
* (once) The Checker stands at the door. -> o_checker
* (once) Somebody learns to read the Vector differently. -> o_readout
* (once) The Vector itself is moved. -> o_encoder
* (if seen == 4) That is all four. -> o_done

== o_none
@add seen 1
# src §9.2: "In the first possible outcome, the diagnostic trace is available but no effective update incorporates it. Retrieval remains unchanged."
Page: In the first possible outcome, the diagnostic trace is available but no effective update incorporates it. Retrieval remains unchanged. ^§9.2
Checker (deadpan): I have filed it. It is on file.
Consumer (smile): I return them both.
-> outcomes

== o_checker
@add seen 1
# src §9.2: "In the second, an external checker filters the altered program while the encoder and similarity scores remain fixed. The combined system improves, but this supplies no evidence of improved discrimination by the embedding."
Page: In the second, an external checker filters the altered program while the encoder and similarity scores remain fixed. The combined system improves, but this supplies no evidence of improved discrimination by the embedding. ^§9.2
Checker (neutral): I do not teach anyone. I stand at the door and take the wrong one out of his hands.
Vector (neutral): The answers are better and I am exactly who I was. I checked. It did not take long.
-> outcomes

== o_readout
@add seen 1
# src §9.2: "A third possibility is that a new consumer learns to use information already present in the fixed vectors. A learned readout may distinguish the programs where the original similarity metric failed."
Page: A third possibility is that a new consumer learns to use information already present in the fixed vectors. A learned readout may distinguish the programs where the original similarity metric failed. ^§9.2
Consumer (thinking): I have not been corrected. I have been replaced, by somebody who reads the same page and sees more on it.
# src §9.2: "If it generalizes and depends on the relevant information in those vectors, the distinction has become usable by that readout without an encoder update. This differs from a checker that independently executes the programs."
Page: If it generalizes and depends on the relevant information in those vectors, the distinction has become usable by that readout without an encoder update. This differs from a checker that independently executes the programs. ^§9.2
Vector (surprised): So it was in me, and nobody had been reading that part.
-> outcomes

== o_encoder
@add seen 1
@fx pulse
# src §9.2: "In a fourth outcome, an encoder update changes the retrieval geometry: the renamed program remains an acceptable match and the altered program falls below the unchanged threshold. The distinction has become usable by the original readout."
Page: In a fourth outcome, an encoder update changes the retrieval geometry: the renamed program remains an acceptable match and the altered program falls below the unchanged threshold. The distinction has become usable by the original readout. ^§9.2
Vector (surprised): That was me. Something in my room is farther off than it was.
# src §9.2: "This is evidence of a change in the encoding, but not necessarily of newly acquired information; the update may reorganize information already present."
Page: This is evidence of a change in the encoding, but not necessarily of newly acquired information; the update may reorganize information already present. ^§9.2
Vector (thinking): Moved, then. Whether I know anything new is apparently a separate question.
-> outcomes

== o_done
# src §1: "Each can improve the answer, but they support different claims about the embedding."
Consumer (smile): The last three can each improve the answer. I would report them as one improvement. ^para
# src §9.2: "A system-level score would group the external-checker and encoder-update cases as improvements. Attributing improved behavioral discrimination to the embedding in both cases would be false."
Page: A system-level score would group the external-checker and encoder-update cases as improvements. Attributing improved behavioral discrimination to the embedding in both cases would be false. ^§9.2
# src §9.2: "We therefore need to ask whether the evidence arrives, whether the system uses it, and what changes as a result."
Page: We therefore need to ask whether the evidence arrives, whether the system uses it, and what changes as a result. ^§9.2
Vector (smile): I could simply learn that pair. The positive one, the negative one. I would never confuse them again.
# src §9.2: "Otherwise an update may have memorized the counterexample or learned to reject every operator change, including harmless ones."
Page: Otherwise an update may have memorized the counterexample or learned to reject every operator change, including harmless ones. ^§9.2
Checker (thinking): And when I say the outputs differ, ask me how I know.
# src §9.2: "The string "different outputs" may be generated without execution, copied from an earlier run, or produced by evaluating these programs under the specified semantics. Its evidential force depends on that connection."
Page: The string “different outputs” may be generated without execution, copied from an earlier run, or produced by evaluating these programs under the specified semantics. Its evidential force depends on that connection. ^§9.2
